        <h2 id="faq" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How long does a car accident settlement take?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Timeline varies enormously. Minor injury claims with clear liability can settle in 4 to 8 weeks after you reach Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI). Moderate injury claims typically take 3 to 6 months. Serious or permanent injuries — especially those requiring surgery — often take 6 to 18 months. If a lawsuit is filed, you're looking at 1 to 3 years before a final resolution. The most important rule: never settle before you have finished medical treatment and know the full extent of your injuries. Read our full guide on <a href="/blog/how-long-does-settlement-take/" class="text-link hover:underline">how long car accident settlements take</a>.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Should I accept the first settlement offer?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Almost never. The first offer from an insurance company is almost always a low-ball figure. Insurance adjusters are trained negotiators whose job is to close claims as cheaply as possible. The first offer often covers only a portion of your medical bills and ignores pain and suffering entirely. You should always counter with a detailed demand letter that outlines all of your damages. If you are not represented by an attorney, at minimum use our calculator to understand the fair value of your claim before responding.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Do I need a lawyer to get a fair settlement?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              For minor accidents with small injuries and clear-cut liability, you may be able to negotiate a reasonable settlement on your own. However, for moderate to serious injuries, having an attorney nearly always results in a significantly higher net recovery — even after the attorney's contingency fee (typically 33%). Attorneys know the true value of claims, understand how to argue for higher multipliers, and are not intimidated by insurance company tactics. If you are unsure, most personal injury attorneys offer free consultations.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              What is a good settlement amount for a car accident?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              A "good" settlement is one that fully compensates you for all of your economic losses — 100% of medical bills and lost wages — plus a fair amount for pain and suffering based on the severity of your injury. As a benchmark: minor injury claims (whiplash, soft tissue) typically settle for $10,000 to $30,000. Moderate injuries (fractures, disc injuries) typically settle for $30,000 to $100,000. Serious or permanent injuries can reach $100,000 to $1 million or more. See our guide on <a href="/blog/average-car-accident-settlement-by-state/" class="text-link hover:underline">average car accident settlement amounts</a> for more context.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Can I negotiate my settlement after an initial offer?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Absolutely — and you should. Settlement negotiation is a standard back-and-forth process. After receiving the insurer's first offer, respond with a counter-demand that is justified by your documentation: itemized medical bills, wage loss statements, your pain journal, and any photos of your injuries. Clearly explain why their offer is too low. Most cases take 2 to 4 rounds of negotiation before reaching a final number. If the insurer refuses to budge, consulting an attorney or filing a lawsuit can shift the dynamic significantly in your favor.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing Paragraph -->
        <p>
          Understanding how car accident settlements are calculated gives you a real advantage. You know what components matter, you know how multipliers work, and you know how fault affects your payout. Armed with this knowledge — and our free calculator — you are in a much stronger position to evaluate any offer you receive and negotiate the compensation you truly deserve.
        </p>

